Work in the complex projective plane $\mathbb{P}^2_{\mathbb{C}}$. A (complex) line arrangement is a finite set $\mathcal{A} = \{L_1, \ldots, L_s\}$ of pairwise distinct lines in $\mathbb{P}^2_{\mathbb{C}}$. For a point $p \in \mathbb{P}^2_{\mathbb{C}}$, the multiplicity of $p$ with respect to $\mathcal{A}$ is the number of lines of $\mathcal{A}$ that pass through $p$. A point of multiplicity exactly $2$ is called a double point, and we write $t_2(\mathcal{A})$ for the number of double points of $\mathcal{A}$.

The known infinite/finite families of arrangements with $s \geq 3$ lines and $t_2(\mathcal{A}) = 0$ include:
(i) the pencil of $s$ lines through a single point;
(ii) the dual Hesse arrangement of $9$ lines (with $12$ triple points);
(iii) the Fermat (or Ceva) arrangements consisting of the $3n$ lines defined by $(x^n - y^n)(y^n - z^n)(z^n - x^n) = 0$ for $n \geq 3$.

Problem. Determine whether there exists a complex line arrangement $\mathcal{A}$ in $\mathbb{P}^2_{\mathbb{C}}$ with $t_2(\mathcal{A}) = 0$ that is not projectively equivalent to one of the arrangements (i), (ii), (iii) above. Equivalently, classify all complex line arrangements with no double points.

